{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1787145509,"nonce":"jsgu5LK89xuf/C0Tjy/z+dH6WfE=","title":"**The demonstration.** `internal/channels/render/slack/reply.go:137-147` builds the `refired` reply body and it carries no link:","message":"```go\ncase reasonRefired:\n    colour = CardFiring.Colour()\n    body = \":repeat: *Re-fired*\"\n    if v.Case != nil {\n        if v.Case.Duration \u003e 0 { body += \" after \" + humanDuration(v.Case.Duration) }\n        body += \" — case #\" + strconv.Itoa(v.Case.Seq)\n    }\n```\n\nThe checked-in capture is the proof, not the reading: `test/fixtures/slack/broadcast_refired.blockkit.json` renders exactly `\":repeat: *Re-fired* — case #1\"` — no `\u003curl|label\u003e`, no `\u003c@U…\u003e`, no `\u003c!date^…\u003e`. Compare the arm five lines above it, `reasonAllResolved`, and the arm that used to sit below it: the deleted `unacked_reminder` body ended `\" — \" + link(v.Links.Group, \"open in oto\")`.\n\n**Why it matters.** `refired` is now **the only reason that broadcasts** (`domain/broadcast.go`, `BroadcastPolicy.Warrants`) apart from the configurable `all_resolved`. A broadcast is by definition the message that leaves the thread and lands in the channel body, so its audience is the people who have **not** been following the thread — and ADR 0020 Amendment 4 established from a live workspace that the in-channel reference carries **no buttons**. So the one message oto sends specifically to un-engaged readers is the one with no affordance and no way back to oto: a reader sees \"Re-fired — case #1\" and has nowhere to click.\n\nThat is a gap the corpus was hiding until now. It surfaced because deleting `unacked_reminder` (git-bug `bd0fb1d`) removed the only broadcast capture, and its replacement is the first broadcast fixture with no mrkdwn control character in it at all — which broke `TestEveryBuilderCaptureIsSomethingBlockKitBuilderWillAccept`'s positive check and made the absence visible.\n\n**Where.** `internal/channels/render/slack/reply.go:137-147` (the body) · `:739-750` (the same reason's `replyFacts` clause, which also adds no link) · `test/fixtures/slack/broadcast_refired.{message,blockkit}.json` (the captures that would change) · `test/harness/slack_goldens_test.go` (the escaping guarantee, see below) · ADR 0020 rule 4, which binds the top-level `text` to be self-sufficient and is the rule this is arguably a corner of.\n\n⚠️ **Not determined:** whether a link belongs in the BODY (as the reminder had it) or in the top-level `text`, and whether `all_resolved` — the other broadcast, when `broadcast_on_resolved` is on — has the same gap. Both were noticed by reading, not by running.\n\n**Scope.** small, but it is a product judgement rather than a mechanical fix, which is why it is a ticket and not a line in `bd0fb1d`. Adding a link changes bytes a human verifies, so it moves two checked-in captures.\n\n⭐ **A second, smaller thing to decide with it.** `TestEveryBuilderCaptureIsSomethingBlockKitBuilderWillAccept` used to assert that EVERY capture contains a mrkdwn `\u003c`, so its unicode-escape check could not pass vacuously. `bd0fb1d` weakened that to a corpus-wide assertion precisely because `broadcast_refired` has none. **If this ticket puts a link in the re-fire, the per-card form can come back** — and it is the stronger check.\n\n**Done when.** Either the `refired` broadcast carries a way back to oto and the two captures are regenerated, or there is a recorded reason why a channel-visible re-fire needs none — and if the first, `slack_goldens_test.go`'s escaping guarantee returns to its per-card form.","files":null}]}